From 7cd66121cedfdbb5c3d752219a6dd1904ceefd46 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TekRantGaming <craigferels896@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 14:51:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Vulkan: resolve readback downscale follows the scaled resolve
 layout

The Vulkan shader gets the addressing 1b52b61 gave the D3D12 one. Scaled
resolves store guest pixels in units of N consecutive pixels (16 bytes, 8
at 8bpp), each becoming scale_x * scale_y host Nx1 subunits in column
major order (XeTextureScaledTiledOffset), but the downscale read each
guest pixel's host pixels as if they were consecutive, so at 2x two of
every four 32 bit pixels came back as a neighbour's value. The source
offset now follows that layout, still counted from source_offset_bytes.

Trials Evolution on Linux at 2x now reads back what it reads at 720p.
Different 32 bit values per copy on the second track, lavapipe in WSL,
with a scripted controller timed by frames so both scales draw the same
frames: texture page requests 22623 against 22686 at 720p, occlusion
images 8829 against 8955 (4735 against 4759 page requests while the
track loads).

Checked on lavapipe against the layout for 8, 16, 32 and 64 bpp at
scales up to 3x3, with and without the half pixel offset. The header was
rebuilt with glslangValidator 15.1.0 and SPIRV Tools 2025.1 (optimize,
remap, disassemble, 8 words a line), which give the previous header back
byte for byte from the previous source.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../shaders/resolve_downscale.cs.glsl         |  34 ++-
 .../vulkan_spirv/resolve_downscale_cs.h       | 277 ++++++++++--------
 2 files changed, 174 insertions(+), 137 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/graphics/shaders/resolve_downscale.cs.glsl b/src/graphics/shaders/resolve_downscale.cs.glsl
index a3c8c12..6d3ce7e 100644
--- a/src/graphics/shaders/resolve_downscale.cs.glsl
+++ b/src/graphics/shaders/resolve_downscale.cs.glsl
@@ -64,25 +64,35 @@ void main() {
   uint scale_xy = xe_downscale_scale_x * xe_downscale_scale_y;
   uint tile_size_scaled = tile_size_1x * scale_xy;
 
-  // Compute offset within each scaled block to sample from.
-  // Without half-pixel correction: sample from (0, 0) = linear offset 0.
-  // With half-pixel correction: sample from (scale/2, scale/2) to compensate
-  // for the D3D9-style half-pixel offset shifting content by (N/2, N/2) pixels
-  // at Nx resolution.
-  uint block_sample_offset = 0u;
+  // The host pixel kept for each guest pixel: (0, 0) of its scale_x * scale_y
+  // block, or (scale/2, scale/2) with half-pixel correction, compensating for
+  // the D3D9-style half-pixel offset shifting content by (N/2, N/2) pixels at
+  // Nx resolution.
+  uint sample_x = 0u;
+  uint sample_y = 0u;
   if (xe_downscale_half_pixel_offset != 0u && scale_xy > 1u) {
-    uint offset_x = xe_downscale_scale_x >> 1u;
-    uint offset_y = xe_downscale_scale_y >> 1u;
-    block_sample_offset = offset_x + offset_y * xe_downscale_scale_x;
+    sample_x = xe_downscale_scale_x >> 1u;
+    sample_y = xe_downscale_scale_y >> 1u;
   }
 
-  // Source offset: base of the scaled block plus offset within block
+  // Scaled resolve layout (XeTextureScaledTiledOffset in texture_address.xesli):
+  // guest pixels are scaled in units of N consecutive pixels (16 bytes, 8 at
+  // 8bpp), each unit becoming scale_x * scale_y host Nx1 sub-units stored
+  // column major. So a guest pixel's host pixels are not consecutive: the kept
+  // one is found from its unit, the sub-unit and the pixel in the sub-unit.
+  uint unit_width_log2 = min(4u - xe_downscale_pixel_size_log2, 3u);
+  uint unit_mask = (1u << unit_width_log2) - 1u;
+  uint unit_index = pixel_index >> unit_width_log2;
+  uint host_x = (pixel_index & unit_mask) * xe_downscale_scale_x + sample_x;
+  uint sub_unit = (host_x >> unit_width_log2) * xe_downscale_scale_y + sample_y;
+  uint unit_size_scaled = (pixel_size << unit_width_log2) * scale_xy;
   // Add source_offset_bytes to handle buffer-relative addressing
   // This must be done before division to preserve sub-dword byte position
   uint src_byte_offset = xe_downscale_source_offset_bytes +
                          tile_index * tile_size_scaled +
-                         pixel_index * pixel_size * scale_xy +
-                         block_sample_offset * pixel_size;
+                         unit_index * unit_size_scaled +
+                         (((sub_unit << unit_width_log2) + (host_x & unit_mask))
+                          << xe_downscale_pixel_size_log2);
   uint src_offset = src_byte_offset >> 2u;
 
   // Destination offset in 1x buffer
